This directory contains two different header-only implementations of a dynamic
array, aka array list, aka vector: `alist.h` and `darray.h`.  `darray.h` uses
macros to define a family of functions for a given type and does the work
inside of those functions. alist.h does not define functions and instead
copies the functionality directly into the substitution site, like sys/queue.h
does on the BSDs and glibc platforms. Both include as much type-checking as C
allows and I could figure out to implement.

The function-family approach used in darray.h is arguably more debuggable,
since gdb can step through those defined functions, but it does have some
drawbacks. For one, the supplied type name must be a single words, so 'struct
foo' is disallowed, but typedef'd structs are permitted. Some projects
discourage matter-of-course struct typedef'ing, because it hides the fact that
a type is a compound type, so this is sometimes a problem.

For Advent of Code, I decided not to pursue these header-only libraries.

Header-only libraries bloat source files, increasing build times and they can
be difficult to debug. These drawbacks are concerning, but perhaps not
especially relevant for AoC solutions which are typcially a fast-compiling
single source file anyway. My primary reason was more ephemeral. Strong
type-checking is not really part C's nature, and trying to bolt it on is
swimming against the current. Header-only libs are also used to avoid dealing
with the linker, but the linker is not scary, and neither is make, at least
not at this scale. void *s are C-nature, so I decided to embrace them. If
strong typing is desired, then it makes more sense to simply switch to C++, or
Rust, or any of the other systems languagues that offer that.